p("immanuel-kant","Immanuel Kant","イマヌエル・カント",1724,1804,["de"],["philosophy"],"Immanuel Kant was a German philosopher whose Critique of Pure Reason is one of the most influential works in the history of philosophy. He synthesized rationalism and empiricism.","Kant was born in Königsberg, Prussia, and spent his entire life in the city. He studied at the University of Königsberg and became a professor of logic and metaphysics.","Kant's critical philosophy revolutionized epistemology, ethics, and aesthetics. His categorical imperative became a cornerstone of moral philosophy, and his work shaped all subsequent philosophy.",[(1770,"Appointed professor at Königsberg"),(1781,"Published Critique of Pure Reason"),(1788,"Published Critique of Practical Reason"),(1790,"Published Critique of Judgment")],[("Critique of Pure Reason",1781,"Investigated the limits and possibilities of human knowledge"),("Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals",1785,"Introduced the categorical imperative as the basis of moral law")],[("Two things fill the mind with ever new and increasing admiration and awe: the starry heavens above me and the moral law within me.","Critique of Practical Reason, 1788")])

p("georg-wilhelm-friedrich-hegel","Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel","ゲオルク・ヴィルヘルム・フリードリヒ・ヘーゲル",1770,1831,["de"],["philosophy"],"Hegel was a German philosopher who developed a comprehensive philosophical framework based on dialectics. His ideas on history, spirit, and the state profoundly influenced Western philosophy.","Hegel was born in Stuttgart, Württemberg. He studied theology and philosophy at the Tübingen Seminary alongside Schelling and the poet Hölderlin.","Hegel's dialectical method and philosophy of history influenced Marx, existentialism, and critical theory. His concept of the dialectic remains central to philosophy.",[(1807,"Published Phenomenology of Spirit"),(1812,"Published Science of Logic"),(1818,"Appointed professor at the University of Berlin"),(1821,"Published Elements of the Philosophy of Right")],[("Phenomenology of Spirit",1807,"Traces the development of consciousness from sense-certainty to absolute knowledge"),("Science of Logic",1812,"Hegel's comprehensive system of dialectical logic")],[("The owl of Minerva spreads its wings only with the falling of the dusk.","Elements of the Philosophy of Right, 1821")])

p("friedrich-nietzsche","Friedrich Nietzsche","フリードリヒ・ニーチェ",1844,1900,["de"],["philosophy"],"Friedrich Nietzsche was a German philosopher whose radical ideas on morality, religion, and the will to power profoundly influenced 20th-century thought.","Nietzsche was born in Röcken, Prussia. He became professor of classical philology at the University of Basel at the remarkably young age of 24.","Nietzsche's critique of traditional morality, his concept of the Übermensch, and his proclamation of the death of God transformed philosophy, literature, and psychology.",[(1872,"Published The Birth of Tragedy"),(1882,"Published The Gay Science"),(1883,"Published Thus Spoke Zarathustra"),(1886,"Published Beyond Good and Evil"),(1889,"Suffered mental collapse in Turin")],[("Thus Spoke Zarathustra",1883,"Philosophical novel introducing the Übermensch and eternal recurrence"),("Beyond Good and Evil",1886,"Critique of traditional morality and philosophical dogmatism")],[("He who has a why to live can bear almost any how.","Twilight of the Idols, 1889")])

p("baruch-spinoza","Baruch Spinoza","バールーフ・デ・スピノザ",1632,1677,["nl"],["philosophy"],"Baruch Spinoza was a Dutch philosopher of Sephardic Jewish origin whose Ethics is one of the greatest works of Western philosophy. He developed a rationalist metaphysical system equating God with Nature.","Spinoza was born in Amsterdam to a Portuguese-Jewish family. He was excommunicated from the Jewish community at age 23 for his radical philosophical views.","Spinoza's pantheistic philosophy and his defense of intellectual freedom influenced the Enlightenment, German idealism, and modern secular thought. Einstein cited Spinoza's God as his own conception.",[(1656,"Excommunicated from the Jewish community"),(1670,"Published Theological-Political Treatise anonymously"),(1677,"Ethics published posthumously")],[("Ethics",1677,"Systematic philosophical work presented in geometric form, equating God with Nature")],[("I have made a ceaseless effort not to ridicule, not to bewail, not to scorn human actions, but to understand them.","Theological-Political Treatise, 1670")])

p("john-locke","John Locke","ジョン・ロック",1632,1704,["gb"],["philosophy","politics"],"John Locke was an English philosopher and physician regarded as the father of liberalism. His political philosophy profoundly influenced the American and French revolutions.","Locke was born in Wrington, Somerset. He studied at Christ Church, Oxford, and became closely associated with Lord Shaftesbury and the Whig movement.","Locke's theory of natural rights, government by consent, and the tabula rasa profoundly influenced democratic thought, the US Constitution, and modern political philosophy.",[(1689,"Published Two Treatises of Government"),(1689,"Published An Essay Concerning Human Understanding"),(1693,"Published Some Thoughts Concerning Education")],[("Two Treatises of Government",1689,"Foundational work on natural rights, property, and government by consent"),("An Essay Concerning Human Understanding",1689,"Major work arguing the mind begins as a blank slate")],[("No man's knowledge here can go beyond his experience.","An Essay Concerning Human Understanding, 1689")])

p("david-hume","David Hume","デイヴィッド・ヒューム",1711,1776,["gb"],["philosophy"],"David Hume was a Scottish philosopher, historian, and economist who is considered one of the most important figures in Western philosophy. He advanced empiricism and skepticism.","Hume was born in Edinburgh, Scotland. He entered the University of Edinburgh at age 12 and later spent time in France developing his philosophical ideas.","Hume's empiricism and skepticism about causation, induction, and the self profoundly influenced Kant, logical positivism, and cognitive science.",[(1739,"Published A Treatise of Human Nature"),(1748,"Published An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding"),(1751,"Published An Enquiry Concerning the Principles of Morals"),(1776,"Published Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion posthumously")],[("A Treatise of Human Nature",1739,"Comprehensive empiricist philosophy covering understanding, passions, and morals"),("An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding",1748,"Refined version of his empiricist epistemology")],[("Reason is, and ought only to be the slave of the passions.","A Treatise of Human Nature, 1739")])

